<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Rajmahal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1576">1576</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajmahal">Rajmahal</a> was fought on 12 July 1576 near Rajmahal on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jharkhand">Jharkhand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, between the Mughal army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> under Khan Jahan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todar-mal">Todar Mal</a> and the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daud-khan-karrani">Daud Khan Karrani</a>, the last sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. Daud was defeated, captured and executed, ending the independent Bengal Sultanate and bringing Bengal into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the death in 1538 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghiyasuddin-mahmud-shah">Ghiyasuddin Mahmud Shah</a>, the last sultan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husain-shahi-dynasty">Hussain Shahi dynasty</a>, the independent sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> came to an end. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a>, the second Mughal emperor, occupied the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Gaur</a> but held it only briefly, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a>, founder of the Sur Dynasty, defeated him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chausa">Battle of Chausa</a> and took control of Bengal. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karrani-dynasty">Karrani dynasty</a> later rose to power there.</p><p>Its last ruler, Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daud-khan-karrani">Daud Khan Karrani</a>, refused to acknowledge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> and was defeated by the Mughal general Munim Khan at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tukaroi">Battle of Tukaroi</a>. The Treaty of Katak of 12 April 1575 left him only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a>, but the peace did not last. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isa-khan">Isa Khan</a>, chief of the Baro-Bhuians, drove the Mughal navy out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, and after Munim Khan's death Daud campaigned to recover his lost lands, conquering North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> sent his general Khan-i-Jahan Husain Quli Beg to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> from Daud Khan, whose generals were Kalapahar, Junaid and Qutlu Khan. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> first defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> at Teliagarhi and captured it, then moved on towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajmahal">Rajmahal</a>. Muzaffar Khan Turbati, the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, came to their aid with five thousand cavalry and supplies. Though the Mughals were the stronger side, they suffered from the climate. The battle was fought at Rajmahal on 12 July 1576. Daud Khan commanded the centre of his army, Junaid the left, Kalapahar the right and Qutlu Khan the front.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karrani-dynasty">Karranis</a> were defeated, and Daud Khan was captured and killed. With the fall of the Karrani dynasty the Bengal Sultanate came to an end and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> became a subah, or province, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>, marking the start of Mughal rule in the region. Resistance continued, however: the Baro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuyan">Bhuyans</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isa-khan">Isa Khan</a> went on fighting the Mughals, who could not gain full control over Bengal until Isa Khan's death in 1599.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Rajmahal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Daud Khan Karrani defied the Mughals, was defeated at Tukaroi in 1575 and was captured and executed after the <b>Battle of Rajmahal</b> in July 1576, ending the sultanate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
